---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc323.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Significant Influence, Not Control" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Significant Influence Indicators" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The 20–50% Rebuttable Presumption" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Equity Method Mechanics" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The Fair Value Option — a US-Specific Flexibility" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Elimination of Unrealised Intercompany Profits" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Losses in Excess of the Investment" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Basic Equity Method Roll-Forward" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example — Losses Exceeding Carrying Amount" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "ASC 323 vs IAS 28 — the Fair Value Option Divergence" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">"One-line consolidation" — reflecting influence without control</div><div class="principle">When an investor has SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE over an investee — meaningful but not controlling power to affect its policies — neither full consolidation nor simple cost/fair-value measurement faithfully represents the relationship. ASC 323's equity method sits deliberately BETWEEN these two extremes, often described as "one-line consolidation": the investor's share of the investee's results flows through a SINGLE investment line and a single income statement line.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">The direct counterpart to IAS 28</div><div class="principle">ASC 323 addresses SUBSTANTIALLY the same fact pattern IAS 28 governs, using a very similar significant-influence threshold and equity method mechanic — one of the more closely converged areas of investment accounting, with the FAIR VALUE OPTION (Section 6) standing out as the principal remaining structural divergence.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Significant Influence, Not Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The threshold that triggers the equity method</div><div class="principle">ASC 323 applies when an investor has the ability to exercise SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE over the operating and financial policies of an investee, but does NOT control it (control would instead trigger CONSOLIDATION under ASC 810) — including investments in common stock and in JOINT VENTURES where the investor shares control jointly with other parties.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Significant Influence Indicators</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A qualitative list, closely mirroring IAS 28's own</div><div class="check-list"><span>Representation on the investee's board of directors</span><span>Participation in the investee's policy-making processes</span><span>Material intercompany (investor-investee) transactions</span><span>Interchange of managerial personnel</span><span>Technological dependency between the investor and investee</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The 20–50% Rebuttable Presumption</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A starting point, not an automatic conclusion</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An investor holding, DIRECTLY or INDIRECTLY, 20% OR MORE of an investee's voting stock is PRESUMED to have significant influence, ABSENT evidence to the contrary — this presumption is REBUTTABLE in both directions. An investor holding LESS than 20% may still demonstrate significant influence through the qualitative indicators in Section 3, and an investor holding MORE than 20% (even up to just under 50%) may demonstrate it does NOT have significant influence despite the shareholding. This is the identical 20% threshold and rebuttable-both-ways structure used under IAS 28.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Equity Method Mechanics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Initial cost, subsequently adjusted</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The investment is initially recognised at COST, then SUBSEQUENTLY ADJUSTED each period to reflect the investor's PROPORTIONATE SHARE of the investee's net income or loss (increasing or decreasing the carrying amount, with the investor's share recognised as income/loss in its own income statement), and REDUCED by dividends received from the investee (treated as a partial return of the investment, not income) — mechanically identical to IAS 28's own equity method.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Fair Value Option — a US-Specific Flexibility</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">An election with no broad IAS 28 equivalent</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">ASC 825 (Financial Instruments) permits an entity to make an IRREVOCABLE ELECTION to measure an otherwise equity-method-eligible investment at FAIR VALUE instead, with changes recognised through NET INCOME — the FAIR VALUE OPTION. IAS 28 does NOT offer an equivalent GENERAL fair value option for ordinary equity method investments; it permits a narrower scope exemption ONLY for investments held by venture capital organisations, mutual funds, and similar entities — a materially narrower carve-out than ASC 825's broadly available fair value option.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Elimination of Unrealised Intercompany Profits</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Consistent with IAS 28's own principle</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The investor's share of INTERCOMPANY profits or losses on transactions with the equity-method investee is ELIMINATED (to the extent of the investor's ownership interest) until realised through a transaction with an outside party — the same substantive conclusion IAS 28 reaches (and the same principle formerly addressed by SIC-3 under IFRS).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Losses in Excess of the Investment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The equity method stops at zero, absent further obligations</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">If the investor's share of investee losses REDUCES the carrying amount of the investment to ZERO, the investor generally DISCONTINUES applying the equity method and does NOT recognise further losses — UNLESS the investor has GUARANTEED obligations of the investee, or is otherwise COMMITTED to provide further financial support, in which case additional losses continue to be recognised to the extent of that commitment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Basic Equity Method Roll-Forward</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A 30% ownership stake</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Investment roll-forward for the year</div><div class="wk-body">An entity purchases a 30% stake in an investee for $3,000,000 at the start of the year. During the year, the investee reports net income of $1,200,000 and pays dividends of $400,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Investor's share of net income = 30% × $1,200,000 = $360,000, recognised as INCOME in the investor's income statement, and ADDED to the investment carrying amount. Investor's share of dividends = 30% × $400,000 = $120,000, RECEIVED as cash and DEDUCTED from the investment carrying amount (not recognised as income). <strong>Closing carrying amount</strong> = $3,000,000 + $360,000 − $120,000 = <strong>$3,240,000</strong>.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Losses Exceeding Carrying Amount</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The equity method suspends at zero</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Severe investee losses</div><div class="wk-body">An investor's equity-method investment carries a balance of $800,000. The investor's share of the investee's loss for the year is $1,100,000. The investor has no guarantee or further funding commitment to the investee.</div><div class="wk-answer">The investor recognises its share of losses only up to $800,000, reducing the investment carrying amount to <strong>ZERO</strong>. The REMAINING $300,000 of the investor's share of losses is NOT recognised (since there is no guarantee or further commitment), and the investor SUSPENDS applying the equity method until the investee reports sufficient future income to offset the unrecognised losses.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 323 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Name of each significant equity-method investee, ownership percentage, and accounting policies</span><span>Summarised financial information (assets, liabilities, results of operations) for material equity-method investees</span><span>Difference, if any, between the carrying amount of the investment and the investor's share of underlying investee equity</span><span>Basis for any fair value option election made under ASC 825</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether the qualitative indicators support (or rebut) the 20-50% presumption of significant influence</span><span>Whether a guarantee or further funding commitment exists, requiring recognition of losses beyond a zero carrying amount</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of the investment, if the fair value option is elected</span><span>Amount of unrealised intercompany profit requiring elimination</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 323 vs IAS 28 — the Fair Value Option Divergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 323 vs IAS 28</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 323)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 28)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Significant influence threshold</span><span>20–50% rebuttable presumption</span><span>Same 20% threshold, rebuttable both ways — fully converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Core equity method mechanics</span><span>Cost, adjusted for share of income/loss, less dividends received</span><span>Identical mechanics</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>General fair value option</span><span>Broadly AVAILABLE via ASC 825, for essentially any qualifying investment</span><span>NOT generally available — a much NARROWER carve-out limited to venture capital/mutual-fund-type entities</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Losses exceeding carrying amount</span><span>Suspended at zero, absent guarantee/commitment</span><span>Same conclusion — fully converged</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The general fair value option's broad availability under US GAAP — but not under IFRS — is the standout structural difference; everywhere else, ASC 323 and IAS 28 are among the most closely aligned standard pairs across the two frameworks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 323 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Initial recognition at cost</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Investment recognised at purchase price</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Investor's share of investee income/loss</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Increases/decreases investment carrying amount; recognised as income/loss</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividends received from investee</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Reduces investment carrying amount; not income</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Fair value option election</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Investment remeasured to fair value through net income each period</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 810</span><span>Consolidation — applies instead where the investor has CONTROL rather than significant influence</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 825</span><span>Supplies the fair value option election available for equity method investments</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 321</span><span>Applies instead where the investor lacks significant influence, holding a passive equity security</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Significant influence</div><div class="glossary-def">The power to participate in an investee's financial and operating policy decisions, without controlling those decisions — the threshold triggering equity method accounting.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">One-line consolidation</div><div class="glossary-def">A descriptive term for the equity method, reflecting how the investor's share of the investee's net assets and results appears as a single line on the investor's own financial statements.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Fair value option</div><div class="glossary-def">An irrevocable election under ASC 825 to measure an otherwise equity-method-eligible investment at fair value through net income instead.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Joint venture</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity owned and operated by a small group of businesses (venturers) as a separate and specific business project, sharing control jointly — within ASC 323's scope alongside ordinary equity method investees.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC323 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
